\title{levygof: goodness-of-fit tests for Lévy degradation processes}
\description{
\code{levygof} tests whether degradation trajectories come from a parametric
Lévy process: gamma (\code{"HGP"}), Wiener (\code{"HWP"}), inverse Gaussian
(\code{"InvGaus"}), variance-gamma (\code{"VG"}) or Meixner (\code{"Meixner"}), with
unknown parameters. The main function is \code{\link[=gof.levy]{gof.levy()}}.
}
\section{Methodology}{

A Lévy process has independent and stationary increments: the increments
over a same time step are i.i.d. The test proceeds in six steps.
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Estimate} the parameters from the observed trajectories by maximum
likelihood (package \code{levyfit}).
\item \strong{Law of the bridge} of the process between two observations (package
\code{levyBridge}).
\item \strong{Simulate the bridge} with the estimated parameters.
\item \strong{Pseudo-sample on a regular grid}: each trajectory is resampled on a
regular grid, with the method \code{"Bridge"} (Grall-Maës, 2012, one grid per
trajectory), \code{"ExtBridge"} (grid containing all the inspection times,
step common to all the trajectories) or \code{"MultiBridge"} (one grid of
Grall-Maës type common to all the trajectories). With several
trajectories, \code{"ExtBridge"} and \code{"MultiBridge"} give one i.i.d. sample
for all the trajectories together. The grid, the step and the drawing of
the bridge of each method are detailed in the section Resampling methods
of \code{\link[=gof.levy]{gof.levy()}}.
\item \strong{Choose a test} for an i.i.d. sample of the law of the increments,
see \code{\link[=tests_disponibles]{tests_disponibles()}}.
\item \strong{Apply the test} to the reconstructed increments.
}

With regular inspections the observed increments are already i.i.d.:
steps 1 to 4 are skipped. \code{\link[=gof.levy]{gof.levy()}} does all the steps;
\code{\link[=test_adequation]{test_adequation()}} does steps 5 and 6 for a sample of increments.
}

\section{Data}{

\code{\link[=gof.levy]{gof.levy()}} accepts the formats of \code{levyfit} and \code{levyBridge}: a data
frame (times, levels) for one trajectory, a list of such data frames for
several trajectories with their own times, or a matrix (times, levels of
each trajectory), as returned by the simulation functions of \code{levysim}.
}

\section{Results}{

\itemize{
\item \code{res} prints the model, the type of inspections, the time step, the
estimated parameters and one row per test;
\item \code{res$results} is the data frame of the tests (statistic, p-value,
critical value, decision);
\item \code{res$bridge} contains the resampled trajectories and \code{res$increments}
the tested increments.
}
}

